[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m A right pyramid has a square base with perimeter 24 inches and a height equal to the side length of the base. If the side length of the base is 6 inches, what is the volume of the pyramid, in inches cubed?
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m To solve this problem, we need to calculate the volume of the right pyramid. The volume \( V \) of a pyramid is given by the formula:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ V = \frac{1}{3} \times \text{Base Area} \times \text{Height} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Given:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - The side length of the square base \( s \) is 6 inches.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - The perimeter of the square base is \( 24 \) inches. We can verify that \( 4 \times 6 = 24 \), which is correct.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - The height of the pyramid \( h \) is equal to the side length of the base, so \( h = 6 \) inches.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m First, we calculate the area of the square base:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ \text{Base Area} = s^2 = 6^2 = 36 \text{ square inches} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Next, we substitute the base area and height into the volume formula:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ V = \frac{1}{3} \times 36 \times 6 \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Let's calculate this using Python to ensure accuracy.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m ```python
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m # Given values
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m side_length = 6  # inches
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m base_area = side_length ** 2  # Area of the square base
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m height = side_length  # Height of the pyramid
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m # Volume of the pyramid
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m volume = (1/3) * base_area * height
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m print(volume)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m ```
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m ```output
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 72.0
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m ```
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m The volume of the pyramid is \(\boxed{72}\) cubic inches.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
