[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m [2026-04-07 06:48:08] [math_rule_reward_worker.py (162)] [ERROR] [reward-math_rule-2 2 / 4][PID 603745] timeout answer: D, response: First, let's evaluate the integral \(I=\int_0^1\frac{\sin x}{\sqrt x}\,dx\). We can use the substitution \(u=\sqrt x\), so \(du=\frac{1}{2\sqrt x}dx\). The limits of integration will change to \(u\) values of \(0\) and \(1\), resulting in \(dx=2u\,du\). The integral becomes \(I=2\int_0^1\sin u^2\,du\). Since \(\sin u^2\) is an even function, we have \(I=4\int_0^{\pi/4}\sin u^2\,du\). Now, we can use the standard approximation \(\sin u^2\approx u^2\) for \(u\) close to \(0\) to obtain \(I\approx 4\int_0^{\pi/4}u^2\,du\approx\frac{4}{3}(\frac{\pi}{4})^3=\frac{\pi^3}{24}\approx\frac{2}{3}\). Since \(\sin u^2>u^2\) for \(0<u<\frac{\pi}{4}\), we have \(I>\frac{2}{3}\).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Next, let's evaluate the integral \(J=\int_0^1\frac{\cos x}{\sqrt x}\,dx\). We can use the substitution \(u=\sqrt x\), so \(du=\frac{1}{2\sqrt x}dx\). The limits of integration will change to \(u\) values of \(0\) and \(1\), resulting in \(dx=2u\,du\). The integral becomes \(J=2\int_0^1\cos u^2\,du\). Since \(\cos u^2\) is an even function, we have \(J=4\int_0^{\pi/4}\cos u^2\,du\). Now, we can use the standard approximation \(\cos u^2\approx 1-\frac{1}{2}u^2\) for \(u\) close to \(0\) to obtain \(J\approx 4\int_0^{\pi/4}\left(1-\frac{1}{2}u^2\right)\,du\approx4\left(\frac{\pi}{4}-\frac{1}{6}(\frac{\pi}{4})^3\right)=\pi-\frac{\pi^3}{24}\approx2\). Since \(\cos u^2<1-\frac{1}{2}u^2\) for \(0<u<\frac{\pi}{4}\), we have \(J<2\). Therefore, the correct answer is (C) \(I>\frac{2}{3}\) and \(J<2\).
