[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 1. \(\log_x(y^x) = 10\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 2. \(\log_y(x^{4y}) = 10\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m First, let's rewrite these logarithmic equations in their exponential forms.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m For the first equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \log_x(y^x) = 10 \implies y^x = x^{10}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m For the second equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \log_y(x^{4y}) = 10 \implies x^{4y} = y^{10}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Now we have two equations:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 1. \(y^x = x^{10}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 2. \(x^{4y} = y^{10}\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Next, we take the natural logarithm of both sides of each equation to simplify them.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m For the first equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \ln(y^x) = \ln(x^{10}) \implies x \ln y = 10 \ln x
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m For the second equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \ln(x^{4y}) = \ln(y^{10}) \implies 4y \ln x = 10 \ln y
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m We now have a system of linear equations in terms of \(\ln x\) and \(\ln y\):
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 1. \(x \ln y = 10 \ln x\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 2. \(4y \ln x = 10 \ln y\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m We can solve this system by expressing \(\ln y\) in terms of \(\ln x\) from the first equation and substituting it into the second equation.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m From the first equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \ln y = \frac{10 \ln x}{x}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Substitute \(\ln y\) into the second equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 4y \ln x = 10 \left(\frac{10 \ln x}{x}\right)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Simplify the equation:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 4y \ln x = \frac{100 \ln x}{x}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Assuming \(\ln x \neq 0\), we can divide both sides by \(\ln x\):
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 4y = \frac{100}{x}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m Solve for \(y\):
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-2) pid=603745)[0m \[
