[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - \(D\) is the intersection of the tangents to \(\omega\) at \(B\) and \(C\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m - \(P\) is the intersection of \(\overline{AD}\) with \(\omega\)
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Since \(D\) is the intersection of the tangents to \(\omega\) at \(B\) and \(C\), \(D\) is the exsimilicenter of the circle \(\omega\) and the point at infinity along the line \(BC\). This implies that \(D\) is the point where the tangents from \(B\) and \(C\) to \(\omega\) meet.
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m By the power of a point theorem, we have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD \cdot AP = AB \cdot AC \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m We need to find \(AP\). Let \(AP = x\). Then:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD \cdot x = AB \cdot AC \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD \cdot x = 5 \cdot 10 \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD \cdot x = 50 \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m To find \(AD\), we use the fact that \(D\) is the exsimilicenter of the circle \(\omega\) and the point at infinity along the line \(BC\). The length \(AD\) can be found using the formula for the length of the tangent from a point to a circle:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD = \frac{AB \cdot AC}{BC} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD = \frac{5 \cdot 10}{9} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ AD = \frac{50}{9} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Now, substituting \(AD\) back into the equation \(AD \cdot x = 50\):
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ \frac{50}{9} \cdot x = 50 \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ x = 50 \cdot \frac{9}{50} \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[ x = 9 \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Thus, \(AP = \frac{50}{9}\).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m The values of \(m\) and \(n\) are 50 and 9, respectively. Therefore, \(m + n = 50 + 9 = 59\).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m The final answer
