[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-1) pid=21765, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m [2026-04-07 10:35:45] [worker.py (135)] [WARNING] [reward-math_rule-1 1 / 4][PID 21765] worker has not strategy[32m [repeated 3x across cluster][0m
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m [2026-04-07 10:36:04] [math_rule_reward_worker.py (162)] [ERROR] [reward-math_rule-3 3 / 4][PID 21766] timeout answer: 23, response: To solve the problem, we need to find the unique point \( C \) on the line segment \( \overline{AB} \) such that \( C \) does not belong to any segment from the family \( \mathcal{F} \) other than \( \overline{AB} \). The coordinates of \( A \) and \( B \) are given as \( A\left(\frac{1}{2}, 0\right) \) and \( B\left(0, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right) \).
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m First, we find the equation of the line \( \overline{AB} \). The slope of \( \overline{AB} \) is:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \text{slope} = \frac{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} - 0}{0 - \frac{1}{2}} = -\sqrt{3}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Using the point-slope form of the equation of a line, the equation of \( \overline{AB} \) is:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m y = -\sqrt{3}x + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m We need to find the point \( C \) on this line such that the distance from \( C \) to the origin \( O \) is the same as the distance from \( C \) to any point \( P \) on the \( x \)-axis and \( Q \) on the \( y \)-axis, where \( \overline{PQ} \) is a unit length segment. Let \( C = (x, y) \). Since \( C \) is on the line \( \overline{AB} \), we have:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m y = -\sqrt{3}x + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m The distance from \( C \) to the origin is:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m OC = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2} = \sqrt{x^2 + \left(-\sqrt{3}x + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\right)^2}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m Simplifying the expression inside the square root:
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \[
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m OC = \sqrt{x^2 + \left(3x^2 - \sqrt{3}x + \frac{3}{4}\right)} = \sqrt{4x^2 - \sqrt{3}x + \frac{3}{4}}
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m \]
[36m(MathRuleRewardWorker(reward-math_rule-3) pid=21766, ip=10.17.70.33)[0m 
